That context changes **intelligibility**, not the main assessment. The guests will understand the references without explanation. It also makes the sister identifiable, so “bitter sister” functions more—not less—as a public accusation.

A diary may record anger without balancing other perspectives. A vow-renewal song is different because it is performed to witnesses and distributed as a permanent account of the marriage. The standard is not “nothing offensive”; it is whether each hard edge serves reconciliation, retaliation, or both.

The strongest hard edges can remain:

- seven months alone;
- the empty house;
- frozen joint accounts;
- the work of repairing the relationship.

The weaker choices are the character judgment about her sister and the condition imposed in the final couplet. They shift the ending from renewed commitment to warning. Elena’s lack of visible reaction still does not establish affirmative consent to performance and distribution.

My recommendation remains: do not perform or distribute this exact version. Preserve the documented events, remove the judgment about the sister, and end with what you are promising rather than what Elena must do.
